      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_9902c441436d',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            dict({
              'definition': 'Registers a function to be called when the application context is popped. The application context is typically popped after the request context for each request, at the end of CLI commands, or after a manually pushed context ends. When the with block exits (or ctx.pop() is called), the teardown functions are called just before the app context is made inactive. Since a request context typically also manages an application context it would also be called when you pop a request context. When a teardown function was called because of an unhandled exception it will be passed an error object. If an errorhandler() is registered, it will handle the exception and the teardown will not receive it. Teardown functions must avoid raising exceptions. If they execute code that might fail they must surround that code with a try/except block and log any errors. The return values of teardown functions are ignored. Changelog Added in version 0.9.',
              'term': 'teardown_appcontext(f)',
            }),
          ]),
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Application Object',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Flask.errorhandler',
            'text': 'errorhandler()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 185,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'teardown_appcontext(f) Registers a function to be called when the application context is popped. The application context is typically popped after the request context for each request, at the end of CLI commands, or after a manually pushed context ends. When the with block exits (or ctx.pop() is called), the teardown functions are called just before the app context is made inactive. Since a request context typically also manages an application context it would also be called when you pop a request context. When a teardown function was called because of an unhandled exception it will be passed an error object. If an errorhandler() is registered, it will handle the exception and the teardown will not receive it. Teardown functions must avoid raising exceptions. If they execute code that might fail they must surround that code with a try/except block and log any errors. The return values of teardown functions are ignored. Changelog Added in version 0.9.',
        'type': 'definition_list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_3a42ad91e49c',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            with app.app_context():
                ...
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Application Object',
        ]),
        'level': None,
