        'page': None,
        'text': 'expires The Expires entity-header field gives the date/time after which the response is considered stale. A stale cache entry may not normally be returned by a cache. Changelog Changed in version 2.0: The datetime object is timezone-aware.',
        'type': 'definition_list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_53a31d35c825',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            dict({
              'definition': 'Enforce that the WSGI response is a response object of the current type. Werkzeug will use the Response internally in many situations like the exceptions. If you call get_response() on an exception you will get back a regular Response object, even if you are using a custom subclass. This method can enforce a given response type, and it will also convert arbitrary WSGI callables into response objects if an environ is provided: This is especially useful if you want to post-process responses in the main dispatcher and use functionality provided by your subclass. Keep in mind that this will modify response objects in place if possible!',
              'term': 'classmethod force_type(response, environ=None)',
            }),
          ]),
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Response Objects',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Response',
            'text': 'Response',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Response',
            'text': 'Response',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Response',
            'text': 'Response',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Response',
            'text': 'Response',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 493,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'classmethod force_type(response, environ=None) Enforce that the WSGI response is a response object of the current type. Werkzeug will use the Response internally in many situations like the exceptions. If you call get_response() on an exception you will get back a regular Response object, even if you are using a custom subclass. This method can enforce a given response type, and it will also convert arbitrary WSGI callables into response objects if an environ is provided: This is especially useful if you want to post-process responses in the main dispatcher and use functionality provided by your subclass. Keep in mind that this will modify response objects in place if possible!',
        'type': 'definition_list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_0a060bfdfadb',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            # convert a Werkzeug response object into an instance of the
            # MyResponseClass subclass.
            response = MyResponseClass.force_type(response)
            
            # convert any WSGI application into a response object
            response = MyResponseClass.force_type(response, environ)
  
          ''',
          'language': None,
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Response Objects',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 494,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          # convert a Werkzeug response object into an instance of the
          # MyResponseClass subclass.
          response = MyResponseClass.force_type(response)
          
          # convert any WSGI application into a response object
          response = MyResponseClass.force_type(response, environ)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
