        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.Flask.secret_key',
            'text': 'Flask.secret_key',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/config/#SECRET_KEY',
            'text': 'SECRET_KEY',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 552,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If you have set Flask.secret_key (or configured it from SECRET_KEY) you can use sessions in Flask applications. A session makes it possible to remember information from one request to another. The way Flask does this is by using a signed cookie. The user can look at the session contents, but can’t modify it unless they know the secret key, so make sure to set that to something complex and unguessable.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_1818dc02cd2e',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Sessions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/flask_api_reference.html#flask.session',
            'text': 'session',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 553,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'To access the current session you can use the session object:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_3adad381781b',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            dict({
              'definition': 'The session object works pretty much like an ordinary dict, with the difference that it keeps track of modifications. This is a proxy. See Notes On Proxies for more information. The following attributes are interesting:',
              'term': 'class flask.session',
            }),
          ]),
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'API',
          'Sessions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
