      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_4e22804b960e',
        'data': dict({
          'kind': 'warning',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://typing.readthedocs.io/en/latest/spec/generics.html#type-variables-with-an-upper-bound',
            'text': 'assignable to the type variable',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 135,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Caution When parametrizing a model with a concrete type, Pydantic does not validate that the provided type is assignable to the type variable if it has an upper bound.',
        'type': 'admonition',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_04876526ffed',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/docs/validation/latest/concepts/config/',
            'text': 'configuration',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/docs/validation/latest/concepts/validators/',
            'text': 'validation',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/docs/validation/latest/concepts/serialization/',
            'text': 'serialization',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 136,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Any configuration, validation or serialization logic set on the generic model will also be applied to the parametrized classes, in the same way as when inheriting from a model class. Any custom methods or attributes will also be inherited.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8a1f73896970',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 137,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Generic models also integrate properly with type checkers, so you get all the type checking you would expect if you were to declare a distinct type for each parametrization.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_2346b8ab1d47',
        'data': dict({
          'kind': 'note',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 138,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note Internally, Pydantic creates subclasses of the generic model at runtime when the generic model class is parametrized. These classes are cached, so there should be minimal overhead introduced by the use of generics models.',
        'type': 'admonition',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_cbd09081b075',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Generic',
            'text': 'Generic',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 139,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'To inherit from a generic model and preserve the fact that it is generic, the subclass must also inherit from Generic:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_23897d0d68d0',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel
            
            TypeX = TypeVar('TypeX')
            
            
            class BaseClass(BaseModel, Generic[TypeX]):
                X: TypeX
            
            
            class ChildClass(BaseClass[TypeX], Generic[TypeX]):
                pass
            
            
            # Parametrize `TypeX` with `int`:
            print(ChildClass[int](X=1))
            #> X=1
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 140,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel
          
          TypeX = TypeVar('TypeX')
          
          
          class BaseClass(BaseModel, Generic[TypeX]):
              X: TypeX
          
          
          class ChildClass(BaseClass[TypeX], Generic[TypeX]):
              pass
          
          
          # Parametrize `TypeX` with `int`:
          print(ChildClass[int](X=1))
          #> X=1
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_c8b97e90dd9c',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 141,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'You can also create a generic subclass of a model that partially or fully replaces the type variables in the superclass:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ceacc29aec89',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel
            
            TypeX = TypeVar('TypeX')
            TypeY = TypeVar('TypeY')
            TypeZ = TypeVar('TypeZ')
            
            
            class BaseClass(BaseModel, Generic[TypeX, TypeY]):
                x: TypeX
                y: TypeY
            
            
            class ChildClass(BaseClass[int, TypeY], Generic[TypeY, TypeZ]):
                z: TypeZ
            
            
            # Parametrize `TypeY` with `str`:
            print(ChildClass[str, int](x='1', y='y', z='3'))
            #> x=1 y='y' z=3
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 142,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel
          
          TypeX = TypeVar('TypeX')
          TypeY = TypeVar('TypeY')
          TypeZ = TypeVar('TypeZ')
          
          
          class BaseClass(BaseModel, Generic[TypeX, TypeY]):
              x: TypeX
              y: TypeY
          
          
          class ChildClass(BaseClass[int, TypeY], Generic[TypeY, TypeZ]):
              z: TypeZ
          
          
          # Parametrize `TypeY` with `str`:
          print(ChildClass[str, int](x='1', y='y', z='3'))
          #> x=1 y='y' z=3
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_bd09c0f5211d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/docs/validation/latest/api/pydantic/base_model/#pydantic.BaseModel.model_parametrized_name',
            'text': 'model_parametrized_name()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 143,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If the name of the concrete subclasses is important, you can also override the default name generation by overriding the model_parametrized_name() method:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8b35d8b88d8f',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Any, Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel
            
            DataT = TypeVar('DataT')
            
            
            class Response(BaseModel, Generic[DataT]):
                data: DataT
            
                @classmethod
                def model_parametrized_name(cls, params: tuple[type[Any], ...]) -> str:
                    return f'{params[0].__name__.title()}Response'
            
            
            print(repr(Response[int](data=1)))
            #> IntResponse(data=1)
            print(repr(Response[str](data='a')))
            #> StrResponse(data='a')
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 144,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Any, Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel
          
          DataT = TypeVar('DataT')
          
          
          class Response(BaseModel, Generic[DataT]):
              data: DataT
          
              @classmethod
              def model_parametrized_name(cls, params: tuple[type[Any], ...]) -> str:
                  return f'{params[0].__name__.title()}Response'
          
          
          print(repr(Response[int](data=1)))
          #> IntResponse(data=1)
          print(repr(Response[str](data='a')))
          #> StrResponse(data='a')
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_f69d0d21b033',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 145,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'You can use parametrized generic models as types in other models:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_742bf4cda8d4',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel
            
            T = TypeVar('T')
            
            
            class ResponseModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
                content: T
            
            
            class Product(BaseModel):
                name: str
                price: float
            
            
            class Order(BaseModel):
                id: int
                product: ResponseModel[Product]
            
            
            product = Product(name='Apple', price=0.5)
            response = ResponseModel[Product](content=product)
            order = Order(id=1, product=response)
            print(repr(order))
            """
            Order(id=1, product=ResponseModel[Product](content=Product(name='Apple', price=0.5)))
            """
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 146,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel
          
          T = TypeVar('T')
          
          
          class ResponseModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
              content: T
          
          
          class Product(BaseModel):
              name: str
              price: float
          
          
          class Order(BaseModel):
              id: int
              product: ResponseModel[Product]
          
          
          product = Product(name='Apple', price=0.5)
          response = ResponseModel[Product](content=product)
          order = Order(id=1, product=response)
          print(repr(order))
          """
          Order(id=1, product=ResponseModel[Product](content=Product(name='Apple', price=0.5)))
          """
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_31834b1d25c2',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 147,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Using the same type variable in nested models allows you to enforce typing relationships at different points in your model:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8dec89f10486',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError
            
            T = TypeVar('T')
            
            
            class InnerT(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
                inner: T
            
            
            class OuterT(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
                outer: T
                nested: InnerT[T]
            
            
            nested = InnerT[int](inner=1)
            print(OuterT[int](outer=1, nested=nested))
            #> outer=1 nested=InnerT[int](inner=1)
            try:
                print(OuterT[int](outer='a', nested=InnerT(inner='a')))  # (1)
            except ValidationError as e:
                print(e)
                """
                2 validation errors for OuterT[int]
                outer
                  Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer [type=int_parsing, input_value='a', input_type=str]
                nested.inner
                  Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer [type=int_parsing, input_value='a', input_type=str]
                """
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 148,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError
          
          T = TypeVar('T')
          
          
          class InnerT(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
              inner: T
          
          
          class OuterT(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
              outer: T
              nested: InnerT[T]
          
          
          nested = InnerT[int](inner=1)
          print(OuterT[int](outer=1, nested=nested))
          #> outer=1 nested=InnerT[int](inner=1)
          try:
              print(OuterT[int](outer='a', nested=InnerT(inner='a')))  # (1)
          except ValidationError as e:
              print(e)
              """
              2 validation errors for OuterT[int]
              outer
                Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer [type=int_parsing, input_value='a', input_type=str]
              nested.inner
                Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer [type=int_parsing, input_value='a', input_type=str]
              """
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_e2b52cf8d374',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 149,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The OuterT model is parametrized with int, but the data associated with the T annotations during validation is of type str, leading to validation errors.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_9f8ae5cb941b',
        'data': dict({
          'kind': 'warning',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance',
            'text': 'isinstance()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance',
            'text': 'isinstance()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 150,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Caution While it may not raise an error, we strongly advise against using parametrized generics in isinstance() checks. For example, you should not do isinstance(my_model, MyGenericModel[int]). However, it is fine to do isinstance(my_model, MyGenericModel) (note that, for standard generics, it would raise an error to do a subclass check with a parameterized generic class). If you need to perform isinstance() checks against parametrized generics, you can do this by subclassing the parametrized generic class:',
        'type': 'admonition',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_1bca9cfc56a1',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            class MyIntModel(MyGenericModel[int]): ...
            
            isinstance(my_model, MyIntModel)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 151,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          class MyIntModel(MyGenericModel[int]): ...
          
          isinstance(my_model, MyIntModel)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_81496ddf0bbb',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 152,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Implementation Details',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_32ba457f3793',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 153,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'When using nested generic models, Pydantic sometimes performs revalidation in an attempt to produce the most intuitive validation result. Specifically, if you have a field of type GenericModel[SomeType] and you validate data like GenericModel[SomeCompatibleType] against this field, we will inspect the data, recognize that the input data is sort of a “loose” subclass of GenericModel, and revalidate the contained SomeCompatibleType data.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_7dfbcf3cd27c',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 154,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'This adds some validation overhead, but makes things more intuitive for cases like that shown below.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_93c8b6760776',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Any, Generic, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel
            
            T = TypeVar('T')
            
            
            class GenericModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
                a: T
            
            
            class Model(BaseModel):
                inner: GenericModel[Any]
            
            
            print(repr(Model.model_validate(Model(inner=GenericModel[int](a=1)))))
            #> Model(inner=GenericModel[Any](a=1))
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 155,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Any, Generic, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel
          
          T = TypeVar('T')
          
          
          class GenericModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
              a: T
          
          
          class Model(BaseModel):
              inner: GenericModel[Any]
          
          
          print(repr(Model.model_validate(Model(inner=GenericModel[int](a=1)))))
          #> Model(inner=GenericModel[Any](a=1))
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_7f4e12918ec0',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 156,
        'page': None,
        'text': "Note, validation will still fail if you, for example are validating against GenericModel[int] and pass in an instance GenericModel[str](a='not an int').",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_14fa084289a2',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 157,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'It’s also worth noting that this pattern will re-trigger any custom validation as well, like additional model validators and the like. Validators will be called once on the first pass, validating directly against GenericModel[Any]. That validation fails, as GenericModel[int] is not a subclass of GenericModel[Any]. This relates to the warning above about the complications of using parametrized generics in isinstance() and issubclass() checks. Then, the validators will be called again on the second pass, during more lax force-revalidation phase, which succeeds. To better understand this consequence, see below:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_23574a4a36a0',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Any, Generic, Self, TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel, model_validator
            
            T = TypeVar('T')
            
            
            class GenericModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
                a: T
            
                @model_validator(mode='after')
                def validate_after(self: Self) -> Self:
                    print('after validator running custom validation...')
                    return self
            
            
            class Model(BaseModel):
                inner: GenericModel[Any]
            
            
            m = Model.model_validate(Model(inner=GenericModel[int](a=1)))
            #> after validator running custom validation...
            #> after validator running custom validation...
            print(repr(m))
            #> Model(inner=GenericModel[Any](a=1))
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 158,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          from typing import Any, Generic, Self, TypeVar
          
          from pydantic import BaseModel, model_validator
          
          T = TypeVar('T')
          
          
          class GenericModel(BaseModel, Generic[T]):
              a: T
          
              @model_validator(mode='after')
              def validate_after(self: Self) -> Self:
                  print('after validator running custom validation...')
                  return self
          
          
          class Model(BaseModel):
              inner: GenericModel[Any]
          
          
          m = Model.model_validate(Model(inner=GenericModel[int](a=1)))
          #> after validator running custom validation...
          #> after validator running custom validation...
          print(repr(m))
          #> Model(inner=GenericModel[Any](a=1))
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_561c8437557d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 159,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Validation of unparametrized type variables',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6448842acc07',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
          'Validation of unparametrized type variables',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-list',
            'text': 'list',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#dict',
            'text': 'dict',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 160,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'When leaving type variables unparametrized, Pydantic treats generic models similarly to how it treats built-in generic types like list and dict:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_99c03b25ee7d',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            'If the type variable is bound or constrained to a specific type, it will be used.',
            'If the type variable has a default type (as specified by PEP 696), it will be used.',
            'For unbound or unconstrained type variables, Pydantic will fallback to Any.',
          ]),
          'ordered': False,
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
          'Generic models',
          'Validation of unparametrized type variables',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://typing.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference/generics.html#type-variables-with-upper-bounds',
            'text': 'bound',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://typing.readthedocs.io/en/latest/reference/generics.html#type-variables-with-constraints',
            'text': 'constrained',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://peps.python.org/pep-0696/',
            'text': 'PEP 696',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Any',
            'text': 'Any',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 161,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If the type variable is bound or constrained to a specific type, it will be used. If the type variable has a default type (as specified by PEP 696), it will be used. For unbound or unconstrained type variables, Pydantic will fallback to Any.',
        'type': 'list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_0bff07b06ba5',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            from typing import Generic
            
            from typing_extensions import TypeVar
            
            from pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError
            
            T = TypeVar('T')
            U = TypeVar('U', bound=int)
            V = TypeVar('V', default=str)
            
            
            class Model(BaseModel, Generic[T, U, V]):
                t: T
                u: U
                v: V
            
            
            print(Model(t='t', u=1, v='v'))
            #> t='t' u=1 v='v'
            
            try:
                Model(t='t', u='u', v=1)
            except ValidationError as exc:
                print(exc)
                """
                2 validation errors for Model
                u
                  Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer [type=int_parsing, input_value='u', input_type=str]
                v
                  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value=1, input_type=int]
                """
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'Models',
