      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_07d276ec503f',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'nargs',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#action',
            'text': 'action',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 145,
        'page': None,
        'text': "If the nargs keyword argument is not provided, the number of arguments consumed is determined by the action. Generally this means a single command-line argument will be consumed and a single item (not a list) will be produced. Actions that do not consume command-line arguments (e.g. 'store_const') set nargs=0.",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_0bd905d90c99',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 146,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'const',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b5e59794210b',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'const',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser',
            'text': 'ArgumentParser',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 147,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The const argument of add_argument() is used to hold constant values that are not read from the command line but are required for the various ArgumentParser actions. The two most common uses of it are:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_23719e353e35',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            "When add_argument() is called with action='store_const' or action='append_const'. These actions add the const value to one of the attributes of the object returned by parse_args(). See the action description for examples. If const is not provided to add_argument(), it will receive a default value of None.",
            "When add_argument() is called with option strings (like -f or --foo) and nargs='?'. This creates an optional argument that can be followed by zero or one command-line arguments. When parsing the command line, if the option string is encountered with no command-line argument following it, the value from const will be used. See the nargs description for examples.",
          ]),
          'ordered': False,
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'const',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.parse_args',
            'text': 'parse_args()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#action',
            'text': 'action',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#nargs',
            'text': 'nargs',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 148,
        'page': None,
        'text': "When add_argument() is called with action='store_const' or action='append_const'. These actions add the const value to one of the attributes of the object returned by parse_args(). See the action description for examples. If const is not provided to add_argument(), it will receive a default value of None. When add_argument() is called with option strings (like -f or --foo) and nargs='?'. This creates an optional argument that can be followed by zero or one command-line arguments. When parsing the command line, if the option string is encountered with no command-line argument following it, the value from const will be used. See the nargs description for examples.",
        'type': 'list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_9b23a69c2f8d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'const',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 149,
        'page': None,
        'text': "Changed in version 3.11: const=None by default, including when action='append_const' or action='store_const'.",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_5f4c0393cf30',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 150,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'default',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ac520890fdce',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 151,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'All optional arguments and some positional arguments may be omitted at the command line. The default keyword argument of add_argument(), whose value defaults to None, specifies what value should be used if the command-line argument is not present. For optional arguments, the default value is used when the option string was not present at the command line:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_11cf79b12415',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=42)
            >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', '2'])
            Namespace(foo='2')
            >>> parser.parse_args([])
            Namespace(foo=42)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 152,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=42)
          >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', '2'])
          Namespace(foo='2')
          >>> parser.parse_args([])
          Namespace(foo=42)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_441e21962017',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 153,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If the target namespace already has an attribute set, the action default will not overwrite it:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6ddd590d3eef',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=42)
            >>> parser.parse_args([], namespace=argparse.Namespace(foo=101))
            Namespace(foo=101)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 154,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=42)
          >>> parser.parse_args([], namespace=argparse.Namespace(foo=101))
          Namespace(foo=101)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_0bb23f971be7',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#type',
            'text': 'type',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.Namespace',
            'text': 'Namespace',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 155,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If the default value is a string, the parser parses the value as if it were a command-line argument. In particular, the parser applies any type conversion argument, if provided, before setting the attribute on the Namespace return value. Otherwise, the parser uses the value as is:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_97f422ed4bd5',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--length', default='10', type=int)
            >>> parser.add_argument('--width', default=10.5, type=int)
            >>> parser.parse_args()
            Namespace(length=10, width=10.5)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 156,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--length', default='10', type=int)
          >>> parser.add_argument('--width', default=10.5, type=int)
          >>> parser.parse_args()
          Namespace(length=10, width=10.5)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_887ba962bd1a',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#nargs',
            'text': 'nargs',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 157,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'For positional arguments with nargs equal to ? or *, the default value is used when no command-line argument was present:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_584c5ff5fb4e',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('foo', nargs='?', default=42)
            >>> parser.parse_args(['a'])
            Namespace(foo='a')
            >>> parser.parse_args([])
            Namespace(foo=42)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 158,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('foo', nargs='?', default=42)
          >>> parser.parse_args(['a'])
          Namespace(foo='a')
          >>> parser.parse_args([])
          Namespace(foo=42)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_5b61139cffdd',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 159,
        'page': None,
        'text': "Because nargs='*' gathers any supplied values into a list, an absent positional argument yields an empty list ([]). Only a non-None default overrides this (so default=None still gives []).",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_c35e6887114f',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#required',
            'text': 'required',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#nargs',
            'text': 'nargs',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 160,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'For required arguments, the default value is ignored. For example, this applies to positional arguments with nargs values other than ? or *, or optional arguments marked as required=True.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_21f1b4b3e832',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 161,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Providing default=argparse.SUPPRESS causes no attribute to be added if the command-line argument was not present:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_68ff7ce36407',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=argparse.SUPPRESS)
            >>> parser.parse_args([])
            Namespace()
            >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', '1'])
            Namespace(foo='1')
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'default',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 162,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', default=argparse.SUPPRESS)
          >>> parser.parse_args([])
          Namespace()
          >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', '1'])
          Namespace(foo='1')
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_022c101fc6ef',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 163,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'type',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ca60bed6bb64',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#float',
            'text': 'float',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#int',
            'text': 'int',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 164,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'By default, the parser reads command-line arguments in as simple strings. However, quite often the command-line string should instead be interpreted as another type, such as a float or int. The type keyword for add_argument() allows any necessary type-checking and type conversions to be performed.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ae715def48c1',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#type',
            'text': 'type',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#default',
            'text': 'default',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 165,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'If the type keyword is used with the default keyword, the type converter is only applied if the default is a string.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_14dd6ee00916',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.register',
            'text': 'register()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentTypeError',
            'text': 'ArgumentTypeError',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/exceptions.html#TypeError',
            'text': 'TypeError',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/exceptions.html#ValueError',
            'text': 'ValueError',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 166,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The argument to type can be a callable that accepts a single string or the name of a registered type (see register()) If the function raises ArgumentTypeError, TypeError, or ValueError, the exception is caught and a nicely formatted error message is displayed. Other exception types are not handled.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_47e4dc3120cb',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 167,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Common built-in types and functions can be used as type converters:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_a000cb1f93a7',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            import argparse
            import pathlib
            
            parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            parser.add_argument('count', type=int)
            parser.add_argument('distance', type=float)
            parser.add_argument('street', type=ascii)
            parser.add_argument('code_point', type=ord)
            parser.add_argument('datapath', type=pathlib.Path)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 168,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          import argparse
          import pathlib
          
          parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          parser.add_argument('count', type=int)
          parser.add_argument('distance', type=float)
          parser.add_argument('street', type=ascii)
          parser.add_argument('code_point', type=ord)
          parser.add_argument('datapath', type=pathlib.Path)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_02058b8098d0',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 169,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'User defined functions can be used as well:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b1d08232f99c',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> def hyphenated(string):
            ...     return '-'.join([word[:4] for word in string.casefold().split()])
            ...
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> _ = parser.add_argument('short_title', type=hyphenated)
            >>> parser.parse_args(['"The Tale of Two Cities"'])
            Namespace(short_title='"the-tale-of-two-citi')
  
          ''',
          'language': 'pycon',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 170,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> def hyphenated(string):
          ...     return '-'.join([word[:4] for word in string.casefold().split()])
          ...
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> _ = parser.add_argument('short_title', type=hyphenated)
          >>> parser.parse_args(['"The Tale of Two Cities"'])
          Namespace(short_title='"the-tale-of-two-citi')
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_f26080c2d6b2',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#bool',
            'text': 'bool()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 171,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The bool() function is not recommended as a type converter. All it does is convert empty strings to False and non-empty strings to True. This is usually not what is desired:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_a9b17a7f1504',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> _ = parser.add_argument('--verbose', type=bool)
            >>> parser.parse_args(['--verbose', 'False'])
            Namespace(verbose=True)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 172,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> _ = parser.add_argument('--verbose', type=bool)
          >>> parser.parse_args(['--verbose', 'False'])
          Namespace(verbose=True)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_39fa3cd5d2bc',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.BooleanOptionalAction',
            'text': 'BooleanOptionalAction',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 173,
        'page': None,
        'text': "See BooleanOptionalAction or action='store_true' for common alternatives.",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6b46f8454c72',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 174,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'In general, the type keyword is a convenience that should only be used for simple conversions that can only raise one of the three supported exceptions. Anything with more interesting error-handling or resource management should be done downstream after the arguments are parsed.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6879fdabd7c6',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/json.html#json.JSONDecodeError',
            'text': 'JSONDecodeError',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/exceptions.html#FileNotFoundError',
            'text': 'FileNotFoundError',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 175,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'For example, JSON or YAML conversions have complex error cases that require better reporting than can be given by the type keyword. A JSONDecodeError would not be well formatted and a FileNotFoundError exception would not be handled at all.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_85b91054afa0',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.FileType',
            'text': 'FileType',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/reference/compound_stmts.html#with',
            'text': 'with',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 176,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Even FileType has its limitations for use with the type keyword. If one argument uses FileType and then a subsequent argument fails, an error is reported but the file is not automatically closed. In this case, it would be better to wait until after the parser has run and then use the with-statement to manage the files.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_58ea13f9fdd9',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'type',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#choices',
            'text': 'choices',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 177,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'For type checkers that simply check against a fixed set of values, consider using the choices keyword instead.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8888ae811457',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 178,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'choices',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_a231edcecd68',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 179,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Some command-line arguments should be selected from a restricted set of values. These can be handled by passing a sequence object as the choices keyword argument to add_argument(). When the command line is parsed, argument values will be checked, and an error message will be displayed if the argument was not one of the acceptable values:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b2954b229fc6',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='game.py')
            >>> parser.add_argument('move', choices=['rock', 'paper', 'scissors'])
            >>> parser.parse_args(['rock'])
            Namespace(move='rock')
            >>> parser.parse_args(['fire'])
            usage: game.py [-h] {rock,paper,scissors}
            game.py: error: argument move: invalid choice: 'fire' (choose from 'rock',
            'paper', 'scissors')
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 180,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='game.py')
          >>> parser.add_argument('move', choices=['rock', 'paper', 'scissors'])
          >>> parser.parse_args(['rock'])
          Namespace(move='rock')
          >>> parser.parse_args(['fire'])
          usage: game.py [-h] {rock,paper,scissors}
          game.py: error: argument move: invalid choice: 'fire' (choose from 'rock',
          'paper', 'scissors')
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6b009d8ab255',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/stdtypes.html#list',
            'text': 'list',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/stdtypes.html#tuple',
            'text': 'tuple',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 181,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Any sequence can be passed as the choices value, so list objects, tuple objects, and custom sequences are all supported.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_d629b04b0159',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/enum.html#enum.Enum',
            'text': 'enum.Enum',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 182,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Use of enum.Enum is not recommended because it is difficult to control its appearance in usage, help, and error messages.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_7e4316a24def',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#type',
            'text': 'type',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#type',
            'text': 'type',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 183,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note that choices are checked after any type conversions have been performed, so objects in choices should match the type specified. This can make choices appear unfamiliar in usage, help, or error messages.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b7188ef1c5af',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#type',
            'text': 'type',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 184,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'To keep choices user-friendly, consider a custom type wrapper that converts and formats values, or omit type and handle conversion in your application code.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_fb1c61a4af61',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'choices',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#metavar',
            'text': 'metavar',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 185,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Formatted choices override the default metavar which is normally derived from dest. This is usually what you want because the user never sees the dest parameter. If this display isn’t desirable (perhaps because there are many choices), just specify an explicit metavar.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b002ff0975b9',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 186,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'required',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8c32dfec8598',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'required',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 187,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'In general, the argparse module assumes that flags like -f and --bar indicate optional arguments, which can always be omitted at the command line. To make an option required, True can be specified for the required= keyword argument to add_argument():',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_691bd9846175',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', required=True)
            >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', 'BAR'])
            Namespace(foo='BAR')
            >>> parser.parse_args([])
            usage: [-h] --foo FOO
            : error: the following arguments are required: --foo
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'required',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 188,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', required=True)
          >>> parser.parse_args(['--foo', 'BAR'])
          Namespace(foo='BAR')
          >>> parser.parse_args([])
          usage: [-h] --foo FOO
          : error: the following arguments are required: --foo
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_845e8ad4e735',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'required',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.parse_args',
            'text': 'parse_args()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 189,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'As the example shows, if an option is marked as required, parse_args() will report an error if that option is not present at the command line.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_d191636cf4d6',
        'data': dict({
          'kind': 'note',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'required',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 190,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note Required options are generally considered bad form because users expect options to be optional, and thus they should be avoided when possible.',
        'type': 'admonition',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_848582d2f2b7',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 191,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'help',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_10cc1cfd5869',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 192,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The help value is a string containing a brief description of the argument. When a user requests help (usually by using -h or --help at the command line), these help descriptions will be displayed with each argument.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_18717be6ee42',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#default',
            'text': 'default',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_argument',
            'text': 'add_argument()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 193,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The help strings can include various format specifiers to avoid repetition of things like the program name or the argument default. The available specifiers include the program name, %(prog)s and most keyword arguments to add_argument(), e.g. %(default)s, %(type)s, etc.:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_235e3f65512b',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='frobble')
            >>> parser.add_argument('bar', nargs='?', type=int, default=42,
            ...                     help='the bar to %(prog)s (default: %(default)s)')
            >>> parser.print_help()
            usage: frobble [-h] [bar]
            
            positional arguments:
             bar     the bar to frobble (default: 42)
            
            options:
             -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 194,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='frobble')
          >>> parser.add_argument('bar', nargs='?', type=int, default=42,
          ...                     help='the bar to %(prog)s (default: %(default)s)')
          >>> parser.print_help()
          usage: frobble [-h] [bar]
          
          positional arguments:
           bar     the bar to frobble (default: 42)
          
          options:
           -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_80ed9097ff43',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 195,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'As the help string supports %-formatting, if you want a literal % to appear in the help string, you must escape it as %%.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_52bb102d5776',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 196,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'argparse supports silencing the help entry for certain options, by setting the help value to argparse.SUPPRESS:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_8dec1b545cfc',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='frobble')
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
            >>> parser.print_help()
            usage: frobble [-h]
            
            options:
              -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'help',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 197,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='frobble')
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', help=argparse.SUPPRESS)
          >>> parser.print_help()
          usage: frobble [-h]
          
          options:
            -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_eb0dd17d06b8',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
        ]),
        'level': 3,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 198,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'metavar',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_81d4576624e8',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser',
            'text': 'ArgumentParser',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#dest',
            'text': 'dest',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#dest',
            'text': 'dest',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#dest',
            'text': 'dest',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 199,
        'page': None,
        'text': "When ArgumentParser generates help messages, it needs some way to refer to each expected argument. By default, ArgumentParser objects use the dest value as the “name” of each object. By default, for positional argument actions, the dest value is used directly, and for optional argument actions, the dest value is uppercased. So, a single positional argument with dest='bar' will be referred to as bar. A single optional argument --foo that should be followed by a single command-line argument will be referred to as FOO. An example:",
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ee9b155c6537',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo')
            >>> parser.add_argument('bar')
            >>> parser.parse_args('X --foo Y'.split())
            Namespace(bar='X', foo='Y')
            >>> parser.print_help()
            usage:  [-h] [--foo FOO] bar
            
            positional arguments:
             bar
            
            options:
             -h, --help  show this help message and exit
             --foo FOO
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 200,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo')
          >>> parser.add_argument('bar')
          >>> parser.parse_args('X --foo Y'.split())
          Namespace(bar='X', foo='Y')
          >>> parser.print_help()
          usage:  [-h] [--foo FOO] bar
          
          positional arguments:
           bar
          
          options:
           -h, --help  show this help message and exit
           --foo FOO
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_06147b0566d8',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 201,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'An alternative name can be specified with metavar:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_50d948ef96ac',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', metavar='YYY')
            >>> parser.add_argument('bar', metavar='XXX')
            >>> parser.parse_args('X --foo Y'.split())
            Namespace(bar='X', foo='Y')
            >>> parser.print_help()
            usage:  [-h] [--foo YYY] XXX
            
            positional arguments:
             XXX
            
            options:
             -h, --help  show this help message and exit
             --foo YYY
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 202,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', metavar='YYY')
          >>> parser.add_argument('bar', metavar='XXX')
          >>> parser.parse_args('X --foo Y'.split())
          Namespace(bar='X', foo='Y')
          >>> parser.print_help()
          usage:  [-h] [--foo YYY] XXX
          
          positional arguments:
           XXX
          
          options:
           -h, --help  show this help message and exit
           --foo YYY
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_33a9b6bd243d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.parse_args',
            'text': 'parse_args()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#dest',
            'text': 'dest',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 203,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note that metavar only changes the displayed name - the name of the attribute on the parse_args() object is still determined by the dest value.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_97b6a1e652dd',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 204,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Different values of nargs may cause the metavar to be used multiple times. Providing a tuple to metavar specifies a different display for each of the arguments:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_bfdcc4825cd7',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='PROG')
            >>> parser.add_argument('-x', nargs=2)
            >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', nargs=2, metavar=('bar', 'baz'))
            >>> parser.print_help()
            usage: PROG [-h] [-x X X] [--foo bar baz]
            
            options:
             -h, --help     show this help message and exit
             -x X X
             --foo bar baz
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'The add_argument() method',
          'metavar',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 205,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='PROG')
          >>> parser.add_argument('-x', nargs=2)
          >>> parser.add_argument('--foo', nargs=2, metavar=('bar', 'baz'))
          >>> parser.print_help()
          usage: PROG [-h] [-x X X] [--foo bar baz]
          
          options:
           -h, --help     show this help message and exit
           -x X X
           --foo bar baz
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_9b0081ae46b3',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
