        'order': 302,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Customizing file parsing',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_6ddec0ae4c3a',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            dict({
              'definition': 'Arguments that are read from a file (see the fromfile_prefix_chars keyword argument to the ArgumentParser constructor) are read one argument per line. convert_arg_line_to_args() can be overridden for fancier reading. This method takes a single argument arg_line which is a string read from the argument file. It returns a list of arguments parsed from this string. The method is called once per line read from the argument file, in order. A useful override of this method is one that treats each space-separated word as an argument. The following example demonstrates how to do this: Note that with this override an argument can no longer contain spaces, since each space-separated word becomes a separate argument.',
              'term': 'ArgumentParser.convert_arg_line_to_args(arg_line)',
            }),
          ]),
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'Other utilities',
          'Customizing file parsing',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/html/python_stdlib_argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser',
            'text': 'ArgumentParser',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 303,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'ArgumentParser.convert_arg_line_to_args(arg_line) Arguments that are read from a file (see the fromfile_prefix_chars keyword argument to the ArgumentParser constructor) are read one argument per line. convert_arg_line_to_args() can be overridden for fancier reading. This method takes a single argument arg_line which is a string read from the argument file. It returns a list of arguments parsed from this string. The method is called once per line read from the argument file, in order. A useful override of this method is one that treats each space-separated word as an argument. The following example demonstrates how to do this: Note that with this override an argument can no longer contain spaces, since each space-separated word becomes a separate argument.',
        'type': 'definition_list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_3102d89b80fb',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            class MyArgumentParser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
                def convert_arg_line_to_args(self, arg_line):
                    return arg_line.split()
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'argparse — Parser for command-line options, arguments and subcommands',
          'Other utilities',
          'Customizing file parsing',
        ]),
        'level': None,
