        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 192,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Path.copy_into(target_dir, *, follow_symlinks=True, preserve_metadata=False) Copy this file or directory tree into the given target_dir, which should be an existing directory. Other arguments are handled identically to Path instance pointing to the copy. Added in version 3.14.',
        'type': 'definition_list',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_423d480f4071',
        'data': dict({
          'items': list([
            dict({
              'definition': 'Rename this file or directory to the given target, and return a new Path instance pointing to target. On Unix, if target exists and is a file, it will be replaced silently if the user has permission. On Windows, if target exists, FileExistsError will be raised. target can be either a string or another path object: The target path may be absolute or relative. Relative paths are interpreted relative to the current working directory, not the directory of the Path object. It is implemented in terms of os.rename() and gives the same guarantees. Changed in version 3.8: Added return value, return the new Path instance.',
              'term': 'Path.rename(target)',
            }),
          ]),
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          'pathlib — Object-oriented filesystem paths',
          'Concrete paths',
          'Copying, moving and deleting',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
