          ...
          What is your name?  It is lancelot.
          What is your quest?  It is the holy grail.
          What is your favorite color?  It is blue.
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_497e689a8922',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#reversed',
            'text': 'reversed()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 115,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'To loop over a sequence in reverse, first specify the sequence in a forward direction and then call the reversed() function.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_b646268e8322',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> for i in reversed(range(1, 10, 2)):
            ...     print(i)
            ...
            9
            7
            5
            3
            1
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 116,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> for i in reversed(range(1, 10, 2)):
          ...     print(i)
          ...
          9
          7
          5
          3
          1
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_e52f6f84817d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#sorted',
            'text': 'sorted()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 117,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'To loop over a sequence in sorted order, use the sorted() function which returns a new sorted list while leaving the source unaltered.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_7ceb5a832429',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> basket = ['apple', 'orange', 'apple', 'pear', 'orange', 'banana']
            >>> for i in sorted(basket):
            ...     print(i)
            ...
            apple
            apple
            banana
            orange
            orange
            pear
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 118,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> basket = ['apple', 'orange', 'apple', 'pear', 'orange', 'banana']
          >>> for i in sorted(basket):
          ...     print(i)
          ...
          apple
          apple
          banana
          orange
          orange
          pear
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_202d3987701c',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/stdtypes.html#set',
            'text': 'set()',
          }),
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/functions.html#sorted',
            'text': 'sorted()',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 119,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Using set() on a sequence eliminates duplicate elements. The use of sorted() in combination with set() over a sequence is an idiomatic way to loop over unique elements of the sequence in sorted order.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_eeb2b25c4820',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> basket = ['apple', 'orange', 'apple', 'pear', 'orange', 'banana']
            >>> for f in sorted(set(basket)):
            ...     print(f)
            ...
            apple
            banana
            orange
            pear
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 120,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> basket = ['apple', 'orange', 'apple', 'pear', 'orange', 'banana']
          >>> for f in sorted(set(basket)):
          ...     print(f)
          ...
          apple
          banana
          orange
          pear
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_25046aa019aa',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 121,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'It is sometimes tempting to change a list while you are looping over it; however, it is often simpler and safer to create a new list instead.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ef1032d6b7db',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> import math
            >>> raw_data = [56.2, float('NaN'), 51.7, 55.3, 52.5, float('NaN'), 47.8]
            >>> filtered_data = []
            >>> for value in raw_data:
            ...     if not math.isnan(value):
            ...         filtered_data.append(value)
            ...
            >>> filtered_data
            [56.2, 51.7, 55.3, 52.5, 47.8]
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.6. Looping Techniques',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 122,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> import math
          >>> raw_data = [56.2, float('NaN'), 51.7, 55.3, 52.5, float('NaN'), 47.8]
          >>> filtered_data = []
          >>> for value in raw_data:
          ...     if not math.isnan(value):
          ...         filtered_data.append(value)
          ...
          >>> filtered_data
          [56.2, 51.7, 55.3, 52.5, 47.8]
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_20acbd2b8a89',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
        ]),
        'level': 2,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 123,
        'page': None,
        'text': '5.7. More on Conditions',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_c95ed3a703f3',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 124,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The conditions used in while and if statements can contain any operators, not just comparisons.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_cc7c17457fa2',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 125,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The comparison operators in and not in are membership tests that determine whether a value is in (or not in) a container. The operators is and is not compare whether two objects are really the same object. All comparison operators have the same priority, which is lower than that of all numerical operators.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_38378f1a4c4f',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 126,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Comparisons can be chained. For example, a < b == c tests whether a is less than b and moreover b equals c.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_f4328008430a',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 127,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Comparisons may be combined using the Boolean operators and and or, and the outcome of a comparison (or of any other Boolean expression) may be negated with not. These have lower priorities than comparison operators; between them, not has the highest priority and or the lowest, so that A and not B or C is equivalent to (A and (not B)) or C. As always, parentheses can be used to express the desired composition.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_70d443055d97',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 128,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'The Boolean operators and and or are so-called short-circuit operators: their arguments are evaluated from left to right, and evaluation stops as soon as the outcome is determined. For example, if A and C are true but B is false, A and B and C does not evaluate the expression C. When used as a general value and not as a Boolean, the return value of a short-circuit operator is the last evaluated argument.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_43cbb08a0f15',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 129,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'It is possible to assign the result of a comparison or other Boolean expression to a variable. For example,',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_25a25f1eb992',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            >>> string1, string2, string3 = '', 'Trondheim', 'Hammer Dance'
            >>> non_null = string1 or string2 or string3
            >>> non_null
            'Trondheim'
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 130,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          >>> string1, string2, string3 = '', 'Trondheim', 'Hammer Dance'
          >>> non_null = string1 or string2 or string3
          >>> non_null
          'Trondheim'
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_ba1e25e22a2d',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.7. More on Conditions',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/faq/design.html#why-can-t-i-use-an-assignment-in-an-expression',
            'text': 'walrus operator',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 131,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note that in Python, unlike C, assignment inside expressions must be done explicitly with the walrus operator :=. This avoids a common class of problems encountered in C programs: typing = in an expression when == was intended.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_5fdbfc163260',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
        ]),
        'level': 2,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 132,
        'page': None,
        'text': '5.8. Comparing Sequences and Other Types',
        'type': 'heading',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_a20c45bff827',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.8. Comparing Sequences and Other Types',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 133,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Sequence objects typically may be compared to other objects with the same sequence type. The comparison uses lexicographical ordering: first the first two items are compared, and if they differ this determines the outcome of the comparison; if they are equal, the next two items are compared, and so on, until either sequence is exhausted. If two items to be compared are themselves sequences of the same type, the lexicographical comparison is carried out recursively. If all items of two sequences compare equal, the sequences are considered equal. If one sequence is an initial sub-sequence of the other, the shorter sequence is the smaller (lesser) one. Lexicographical ordering for strings uses the Unicode code point number to order individual characters. Some examples of comparisons between sequences of the same type:',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_577aee2c6e44',
        'data': dict({
          'code': '''
            (1, 2, 3)              < (1, 2, 4)
            [1, 2, 3]              < [1, 2, 4]
            'ABC' < 'C' < 'Pascal' < 'Python'
            (1, 2, 3, 4)           < (1, 2, 4)
            (1, 2)                 < (1, 2, -1)
            (1, 2, 3)             == (1.0, 2.0, 3.0)
            (1, 2, ('aa', 'ab'))   < (1, 2, ('abc', 'a'), 4)
  
          ''',
          'language': 'python3',
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.8. Comparing Sequences and Other Types',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
        ]),
        'order': 134,
        'page': None,
        'text': '''
          (1, 2, 3)              < (1, 2, 4)
          [1, 2, 3]              < [1, 2, 4]
          'ABC' < 'C' < 'Pascal' < 'Python'
          (1, 2, 3, 4)           < (1, 2, 4)
          (1, 2)                 < (1, 2, -1)
          (1, 2, 3)             == (1.0, 2.0, 3.0)
          (1, 2, ('aa', 'ab'))   < (1, 2, ('abc', 'a'), 4)
  
        ''',
        'type': 'code',
      }),
      dict({
        'block_id': 'b_64baa1fbd526',
        'data': dict({
        }),
        'heading_path': list([
          '5. Data Structures',
          '5.8. Comparing Sequences and Other Types',
        ]),
        'level': None,
        'links': list([
          dict({
            'href': 'https://fixtures.nina.test/builtins/exceptions.html#TypeError',
            'text': 'TypeError',
          }),
        ]),
        'order': 135,
        'page': None,
        'text': 'Note that comparing objects of different types with < or > is legal provided that the objects have appropriate comparison methods. For example, mixed numeric types are compared according to their numeric value, so 0 equals 0.0, etc. Otherwise, rather than providing an arbitrary ordering, the interpreter will raise a TypeError exception.',
        'type': 'paragraph',
      }),
      dict({
