# name: test_fixture_markdown_snapshot[mkdocs_material_code_blocks]
  '''
  # Code blocks
  
  Code blocks and examples are an essential part of technical project documentation. Material for MkDocs provides different ways to set up syntax highlighting for code blocks, either during build time using Pygments or during runtime using a JavaScript syntax highlighter.
  
  ## Configuration
  
  This configuration enables syntax highlighting on code blocks and inline code blocks, and allows to include source code directly from other files. Add the following lines to mkdocs.yml:
  
  ```yaml
  markdown_extensions:
    - pymdownx.highlight:
        anchor_linenums: true
        line_spans: __span
        pygments_lang_class: true
    - pymdownx.inlinehilite
    - pymdownx.snippets
    - pymdownx.superfences
  ```
  
  The following sections discuss how to use different syntax highlighting features with Pygments, the recommended highlighter, so they don't apply when using a JavaScript syntax highlighter.
  
  See additional configuration options:
  
  - Highlight
  - InlineHilite
  - SuperFences
  - Snippets
  
  ### Code copy button
  
  9.0.0
  
  Code blocks can automatically render a button on the right side to allow the user to copy a code block's contents to the clipboard. Add the following to mkdocs.yml to enable them globally:
  
  ```yaml
  theme:
    features:
      - content.code.copy
  ```
  
  > [!IMPORTANT]
  > Enabling or disabling code copy buttons for a specific code block If you don't want to enable code copy buttons globally, you can enable them for a specific code block by using a slightly different syntax based on the Attribute Lists extension: Note that there must be a language shortcode, which has to come first and must also be prefixed by a .. Similarly, the copy button can also be disabled for a specific code block: To enable or disable the copy button without syntax highlighting, you can use the .text language shortcode, which doesn't highlight anything.
  
  ```yaml
  ``` { .yaml .copy }
  # Code block content
  ```
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  ``` { .yaml .no-copy }
  # Code block content
  ```
  ```
  
  ### Code selection button
  
  9.7.0
  
  Code blocks can include a button to allow for the selection of line ranges by the user, which is perfect for linking to a specific subsection of a code block. This allows the user to apply line highlighting dynamically. Add the following to mkdocs.yml to enable it globally:
  
  ```yaml
  theme:
    features:
      - content.code.select
  ```
  
  > [!IMPORTANT]
  > Enabling or disabling code selection buttons for a specific code block If you don't want to enable code selection buttons globally, you can enable them for a specific code block by using a slightly different syntax based on the Attribute Lists extension: Note that the language shortcode which has to come first must now also be prefixed by a .. Similarly, the selection button can also be disabled for a specific code block:
  
  ```yaml
  ``` { .yaml .select }
  # Code block content
  ```
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  ``` { .yaml .no-select }
  # Code block content
  ```
  ```
  
  ### Code annotations
  
  8.0.0
  
  Code annotations offer a comfortable and friendly way to attach arbitrary content to specific sections of code blocks by adding numeric markers in block and inline comments in the language of the code block. Add the following to mkdocs.yml to enable them globally:
  
  ```yaml
  theme:
    features:
      - content.code.annotate # (1)!
  ```
  
  1. I'm a code annotation! I can contain code, formatted text, images, ... basically anything that can be written in Markdown.
  
  > [!IMPORTANT]
  > Enabling code annotations for a specific code block If you don't want to enable code annotations globally, because you don't like the automatic inlining behavior, you can enable them for a specific code block by using a slightly different syntax based on the Attribute Lists extension: Note that the language shortcode which has to come first must now also be prefixed by a ..
  
  ```yaml
  ``` { .yaml .annotate }
  # Code block content
  ```
  ```
  
  #### Custom selectors
  
  9.7.0
  
  Normally, code annotations can only be placed in comments, as comments can be considered safe for placement. However, sometimes it might be necessary to place annotations in parts of the code block where comments are not allowed, e.g. in strings.
  
  Additional selectors can be set per-language:
  
  ```yaml
  extra:
    annotate:
      json: [.s2] # (1)!
  ```
  
  1. .s2 is the name of the lexeme that Pygments generates for double-quoted strings. If you want to use a code annotation in another lexeme than a comment, inspect the code block and determine which lexeme needs to be added to the list of additional selectors. Important: Code annotations cannot be split between lexemes.
  
  Now, code annotations can be used from within strings in JSON:
  
  ```json
  {
    "key": "value (1)"
  }
  ```
  
  1. I'm a code annotation! I can contain code, formatted text, images, ... basically anything that can be written in Markdown.
  
  ## Usage
  
  Code blocks must be enclosed with two separate lines containing three backticks. To add syntax highlighting to those blocks, add the language shortcode directly after the opening block. See the list of available lexers to find the shortcode for a given language:
  
  Code block
  
  ```markdown
  ``` py
  import tensorflow as tf
  ```
  ```
  
  ```py
  import tensorflow as tf
  ```
  
  ### Adding a title
  
  In order to provide additional context, a custom title can be added to a code block by using the title="<custom title>" option directly after the shortcode, e.g. to display the name of a file:
  
  Code block with title
  
  ```markdown
  ``` py title="bubble_sort.py"
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  ```
  
  bubble_sort.py
  
  ```py
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  
  ### Adding annotations
  
  Code annotations can be placed anywhere in a code block where a comment for the language of the block can be placed, e.g. for JavaScript in // ... and /* ... */, for YAML in # ..., etc.1:
  
  Code block with annotation
  
  ```markdown
  ``` yaml
  theme:
    features:
      - content.code.annotate # (1)
  ```
  
  1.  :man_raising_hand: I'm a code annotation! I can contain `code`, __formatted
      text__, images, ... basically anything that can be written in Markdown.
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  theme:
    features:
      - content.code.annotate # (1)
  ```
  
  1. I'm a code annotation! I can contain code, formatted text, images, ... basically anything that can be written in Markdown.
  
  #### Stripping comments
  
  8.5.0
  
  If you wish to strip the comment characters surrounding a code annotation, simply add an ! after the closing parenthesis of the code annotation:
  
  Code block with annotation, stripped
  
  ```markdown
  ``` yaml
  # (1)!
  ```
  
  1.  Look ma, less line noise!
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  # (1)!
  ```
  
  1. Look ma, less line noise!
  
  Note that this only allows for a single code annotation to be rendered per comment. If you want to add multiple code annotations, comments cannot be stripped for technical reasons.
  
  ### Adding line numbers
  
  Line numbers can be added to a code block by using the linenums="<start>" option directly after the shortcode, whereas <start> represents the starting line number. A code block can start from a line number other than 1, which allows to split large code blocks for readability:
  
  Code block with line numbers
  
  ```markdown
  ``` py linenums="1"
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  ```
  
  ```
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  
  ### Highlighting specific lines
  
  Specific lines can be highlighted by passing the line numbers to the hl_lines argument placed right after the language shortcode. Note that line counts start at 1, regardless of the starting line number specified as part of linenums:
  
  Lines
  
  Line ranges
  
  Code block with highlighted lines
  
  ```markdown
  ``` py hl_lines="2 3"
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  ```
  
  ```
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  
  Code block with highlighted line range
  
  ```markdown
  ``` py hl_lines="3-5"
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  ```
  
  ```
  def bubble_sort(items):
      for i in range(len(items)):
          for j in range(len(items) - 1 - i):
              if items[j] > items[j + 1]:
                  items[j], items[j + 1] = items[j + 1], items[j]
  ```
  
  ### Highlighting inline code blocks
  
  When InlineHilite is enabled, syntax highlighting can be applied to inline code blocks by prefixing them with a shebang, i.e. #!, directly followed by the corresponding language shortcode.
  
  Inline code block
  
  ```markdown
  The `#!python range()` function is used to generate a sequence of numbers.
  ```
  
  The range() function is used to generate a sequence of numbers.
  
  ### Embedding external files
  
  When Snippets is enabled, content from other files (including source files) can be embedded by using the --8<-- notation directly from within a code block:
  
  Code block with external content
  
  ```markdown
  ``` title=".browserslistrc"
  --8<-- ".browserslistrc"
  ```
  ```
  
  .browserslistrc
  
  ```text
  last 4 years
  ```
  
  ## Customization
  
  ### Custom syntax theme
  
  If Pygments is used, Material for MkDocs provides the styles for code blocks, which are built with a custom and well-balanced palette that works equally well for both color schemes:
  
  - --md-code-hl-number-color
  - --md-code-hl-special-color
  - --md-code-hl-function-color
  - --md-code-hl-constant-color
  - --md-code-hl-keyword-color
  - --md-code-hl-string-color
  - --md-code-hl-name-color
  - --md-code-hl-operator-color
  - --md-code-hl-punctuation-color
  - --md-code-hl-comment-color
  - --md-code-hl-generic-color
  - --md-code-hl-variable-color
  
  Code block foreground, background and line highlight colors are defined via:
  
  - --md-code-fg-color
  - --md-code-bg-color
  - --md-code-hl-color
  
  Let's say you want to change the color of "strings". While there are several types of string tokens, they use the same color. You can assign a new color by using an additional style sheet:
  
  docs/stylesheets/extra.css
  
  mkdocs.yml
  
  ```css
  :root > * {
    --md-code-hl-string-color: #0FF1CE;
  }
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  extra_css:
    - stylesheets/extra.css
  ```
  
  If you want to tweak a specific type of string, e.g. `backticks`, you can lookup the specific CSS class name in the syntax theme definition, and override it as part of your additional style sheet:
  
  docs/stylesheets/extra.css
  
  mkdocs.yml
  
  ```css
  .highlight .sb {
    color: #0FF1CE;
  }
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  extra_css:
    - stylesheets/extra.css
  ```
  
  ### Annotation tooltip width
  
  If you have a lot of content hosted inside your code annotations, it can be a good idea to increase the width of the tooltip by adding the following as part of an additional style sheet:
  
  docs/stylesheets/extra.css
  
  mkdocs.yml
  
  ```css
  :root {
    --md-tooltip-width: 600px;
  }
  ```
  
  ```yaml
  extra_css:
    - stylesheets/extra.css
  ```
  
  This will render annotations with a larger width:
  
  ```yaml
  # (1)!
  ```
  
  1. Muuuuuuuuuuuuuuuch more space for content
  
  1. Code annotations require syntax highlighting with Pygments – they're currently not compatible with JavaScript syntax highlighters, or languages that do not have comments in their grammar. However, we're actively working on supporting alternate ways of defining code annotations, allowing to always place code annotations at the end of lines. ↩
  '''
# ---
# name: test_fixture_markdown_snapshot[pydantic_changelog]
  '''
  # Changelog
  
  ## v2.14.0 (2026-10-08)
  
  GitHub release
  
  ### What’s Changed
  
  The highlights of the v2.14 release are available in the blog post. Several minor changes (considered non-breaking changes according to our versioning policy) are also included in this release. Make sure to look into them before upgrading.
  
  This release drops support for Python 3.9 and adds support for Python 3.15.
  
  #### New Features
  
  - Add support for multiple_of constraint in fraction and timedelta core schemas by @Viicos in #13873
  - Add __namespace__ parameter to create_model() by @Viicos in #13895
  
  #### Changes
  
  - Add an ordered-dict core schema by @Viicos in #13796
  - Add a counter core schema by @Viicos in #13824
  - Do not mutate model_config attribute by @Viicos in #13825
  - Reject newlines in NameEmail validation by @Viicos in #13897
  
  #### Fixes
  
  - Call enum constructors in both validation modes by @Viicos in #13792
  - Fix documentation examples not raising and remove unused string_sub_type unused error code by @Viicos in #13821
  - Catch OverflowError during ByteSize validation by @Viicos in #13850
  - Require multiple_of constraints to be positive by @Viicos in #13862
  - Always pop field name and model type stacks during schema generation by @saquibjawedbit in #13859
  - Handle TypeError gracefully during docstring extraction by @Viicos in #13874
  - Fix config propagation of stdlib dataclasses and TypedDicts in JSON Schema by @Viicos in #13891
  - Don’t apply ser_json_timedelta to all datetime types in serialization inference by @Viicos in #13892
  - Encode JSON Schema defaults with a consistent configuration by @Viicos in #13893
  - Propagate init and kw_only=False from Field() to stdlib dataclass fields by @Viicos in #13898
  - Respect propagated extra config in the JSON Schema of stdlib dataclasses by @Viicos in #13899
  - Handle OS errors during ZoneInfo validation by @Viicos in #13912
  - Handle exceptions during Decimal validation by @Viicos in #13940
  - Rebuild incomplete types during serialization inference by @Viicos in #13922
  - Reject large exponents in Fraction validation by @Viicos in #13944
  - Avoid validating URL prefix multiple times in multi host URLs by @Viicos in #13945
  
  ### New Contributors
  
  - @saquibjawedbit made their first contribution in #13859
  
  ## v2.14.0b2 (2026-09-09)
  
  GitHub release
  
  ### What’s Changed
  
  This pre-release adds full support for Python 3.15 specific features, such as support for frozendict, making use of TypeForm and stabilizing the MISSING sentinel type.
  
  #### New Features
  
  - Allow any CoreSchema to be used as serialization by @Viicos in #13680
  - Add support for frozendict type by @Viicos in #13634
  - Allow dict, frozendict and frozenset as defaultdict default factories by @Viicos in #13623
  - Add support for TypeForm by @Viicos in #13459. This adds support for static type checking where a type form is expected. For instance, type adapters:
  - Add support for lazy imports by @Viicos in #13776
  - Stabilize MISSING sentinel by @Viicos in #13782. The MISSING sentinel is no longer experimental, and can be imported as from pydantic import MISSING.
  
  ```python
  from pydantic import TypeAdapter
  
  ta = TypeAdapter(int | str)
  assert_type(ta.validate_python(1), int | str)
  ```
  
  #### Changes
  
  - Take ser_json_temporal into account when generating JSON Schema by @shashiKundur1 in #13665
  - Use the field name in __signature__ when validate_by_alias isFalse by @jaideeppyne in #13730
  - Add a deque core schema by @Viicos in #13757
  - Do not mention MISSING as a valid value in validation errors by @Viicos in #13779
  - Refactor Decimal JSON Schema pattern constraint by @Viicos in #13672
  
  #### Performance
  
  - Refactor type references logic by @Viicos in #13643
  - Improve performance of GenerateSchema.generate_schema() dispatching by @Viicos in #13614
  - Move schema gathering logic to pydantic-core by @Viicos in #13725
  - Improve performance of FieldInfo construction by @Viicos in #13726
  
  #### Fixes
  
  - Fix support for callable discriminators with PEP 695 type aliases by @Viicos in #13604
  - Preserve re.Pattern instance for compiled patterns in the experimental pipeline by @soma0212 in #13611
  - Fix missing GC traversal on some pydantic-core struct fields by @Viicos in #13624
  - Fix missing GC traversal in pydantic-core for GeneralFieldsSerializer by @Viicos in #13629
  - Fix JSON schema discriminator mapping keys for bool discriminators by @remi-fongaufier in #13632
  - Fix application of other constraints in the pipeline API by @Viicos in #13659
  - Support bare None annotation for discriminated unions by @Viicos in #13667
  - Respect runtime alias validation configuration when wrap validators are present by @Viicos in #13668
  - Exclude computed fields with exclude_if from JSON Schema required fields by @Bishwas-py in #13563
  - Use config of TypeAdapter in JSON Schema generation by @Viicos in #13676
  - Support populate_by_name in @validate_call by @Viicos in #13691
  - Allow None to serialize against secret fields by @Viicos in #13699
  - Do not drop include/exclude if serializer wasn’t called by @Viicos in #13702
  - Don’t apply serialization temporal formats to validation JSON schemas by @MLuc24 in #13711
  - Reflect str_min_length and str_max_length config in the JSON schema by @MLuc24 in #13714
  - Treat a class attribute set to None as existing when assigning with extra='allow' by @Kropiunig in #13718
  - Use the field name in validation JSON schemas when validate_by_alias is False by @MLuc24 in #13717
  - Do not rely on normalization to validate decimals by @Viicos in #13742
  - Handle any exception when trying to compute JSON Schema default by @Viicos in #13741
  - Fix Mypy plugin crash with import cycle by @Viicos in #13767
  - Fix supported constraints for types of datetime module by @Viicos in #13768
  - Fix inconsistencies between constraints and core schemas allowed values by @Viicos in #13772
  - Allow self to be used as a parameter with @validate_call by @Viicos in #13773
  - Fix race conditions in freethreaded build by @Viicos in #13785
  
  ### New Contributors
  
  - @soma0212 made their first contribution in #13611
  - @remi-fongaufier made their first contribution in #13632
  - @shashiKundur1 made their first contribution in #13665
  - @MLuc24 made their first contribution in #13711
  - @jaideeppyne made their first contribution in #13730
  
  ## v2.14.0b1 (2026-08-06)
  
  GitHub release
  
  ### What’s Changed
  
  This is the first beta release of the 2.14 version, providing some new features and performance improvements for model builds.
  
  This release is the first to provide initial support for Python 3.15, although not all 3.15 specific features are supported yet.
  
  #### Packaging
  
  - Add agent skills by @Viicos in #13193
  
  #### New Features
  
  - Add support for EllipsisType by @Viicos in #13484
  - Add initial support for Python 3.15 by @Viicos in #13587
  
  #### Changes
  
  - Populate __slots__ from generic class during parameterization by @Viicos in #13243
  - Avoid using explicit Any in the Mypy plugin by @Viicos in #13196
  - Support Fraction type in pydantic-core by @Viicos in #13339
  - Use constant-time comparison for SecretStr and SecretBytes types by @Viicos in #13518
  - Fix application of constraints in pipeline API by @Viicos in #13516
  - Optimize type lookup logic in core schema generation by @Viicos in #13573
  - Add a named-tuple core schema by @Viicos in #13505
  - Raise explicit TypeError on invalid index_key serialization wrap handler by @Viicos in #13506
  
  #### Performance
  
  - Cache ModelPrivateAttr.default_factory_takes_validated_data property by @Viicos in #13390
  - Introduce micro-optimizations for model class building by @dmontagu in #13540
  - Try Format.VALUE before FORWARDREF when evaluating annotations in ModelMetaclass by @Viicos in #13584
  - Optimize type lookup logic in core schema generation by @Viicos in #13573
  - Avoid exponential core schema traversal in gather_schemas_for_cleaning() by @dmontagu in #13523
  
  #### Fixes
  
  - Validate falsy AliasGenerator outputs by @jiyujie2006 in #13248
  - Do not resolve recursive 'definitions-ref' schemas in schema cleaning by @Viicos in #13276
  - Fix missing max_length check on lazy iteration validation by @davidhewitt in #13278
  - Handle missing errors with default factories taking validated data by @Viicos in #13291
  - Recognise WindowsPath as a schema type by @davidhewitt in #13326
  - Fix application of nested operators in pipeline API by @Viicos in #13363
  - Fix type variable substitution in __pydantic_extra__ by @Viicos in #13391
  - Fix json_schema_extra dict being dropped when a callable follows it in Annotated by @Srivatsa03 in #13374
  - Skip RootModel.__init__() in Mypy plugin by @Viicos in #13441
  - Defer placeholders in Mypy plugin by @Viicos in #13442
  - Do not ignore timezone offset when using 'seconds'/'milliseconds' temporal_mode by @Viicos in #13436
  - Make model rebuilding logic thread safe by @Viicos in #13438
  - Fix Interval zero bounds silently dropped in pipeline API by @pctablet505 in #13452
  - Sync RootModel.model_dump() signature with its parent by @Viicos in #13456
  - Return NotImplemented from URL ordering comparisons with foreign types by @chinesepowered in #13495
  - Don’t error on unhashable generic arguments on generic Pydantic models by @Viicos in #13515
  - Fix population of SerializationInfo.exclude_computed_fields by @Viicos in #13560
  - Fix serialization of ValidateAs by @Viicos in #13487
  - Allow reuse of validators when plugins are set by @Viicos in #13535
  - Avoid double-wrapping polymorphism trampoline for model serializers by @davidhewitt in #13552
  - Add AllowInfNan type to the field metadata gathering by @mberdyshev in #13586
  
  ### New Contributors
  
  - @jiyujie2006 made their first contribution in #13248
  - @pctablet505 made their first contribution in #13452
  - @LuShadowX made their first contribution in #13503
  - @mberdyshev made their first contribution in #13586
  
